---
import ArticleLayout from '../../components/ArticleLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How long does an HVAC system last?',
    a: 'A central air conditioner or heat pump typically lasts 12 to 15 years, and a gas furnace 15 to 20 years, in the Middle Tennessee climate. Systems that get annual service reliably reach the top of that range; neglected ones fail years early.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does a roof last?',
    a: 'A standard asphalt shingle roof lasts about 15 to 25 years, architectural shingles 25 to 30, and metal roofing 40 or more. Middle Tennessee storms and heat shorten shingle life, so an annual inspection matters here.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does a water heater last?',
    a: 'A conventional tank water heater lasts 8 to 12 years; a tankless unit 15 to 20. Flushing sediment once a year is the single biggest factor in reaching the high end.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does LVP flooring last in a rental?',
    a: 'Luxury vinyl plank lasts 15 to 25 years depending on the thickness of its wear layer, which is why it has become the standard rental flooring. It routinely outlasts several tenancies where carpet would need replacing every 5 to 8 years.',
  },
];
---
<ArticleLayout
  slug="how-long-do-home-systems-last"
  eyebrow="Reference Guide"
  title="How Long Do Major Home Systems Last? (HVAC, Roof, Water Heater & More)"
  description="Typical lifespans for the expensive systems in a rental or home: HVAC, roof, water heater, and flooring, plus the signs each one is near the end."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    The expensive systems in a property fail on a schedule you can plan for.
    An HVAC system lasts <strong>12 to 15 years</strong>, a tank water heater
    <strong>8 to 12</strong>, an asphalt roof <strong>15 to 25</strong>, and
    LVP flooring <strong>15 to 25</strong>. Knowing where each system sits in
    its lifespan is the difference between a planned replacement and a
    midnight emergency. Here is the full reference, with the first sign each
    one is nearing the end.
  </p>

  <h2>Lifespan reference table</h2>
  <p>
    Typical service life for the major systems and finishes in a Middle
    Tennessee home or rental. Ranges assume normal use; regular maintenance
    pushes toward the top, neglect toward the bottom.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>System</th><th>Typical lifespan</th><th>First sign it is aging</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Central AC / heat pump</td><td>12 – 15 years</td><td>Rising bills, weak cooling, frequent repairs</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gas furnace</td><td>15 – 20 years</td><td>Short cycling, uneven heat, yellow burner flame</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Water heater (tank)</td><td>8 – 12 years</td><td>Rusty water, popping sounds, moisture at the base</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Water heater (tankless)</td><td>15 – 20 years</td><td>Fluctuating temps, error codes, scale buildup</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Asphalt shingle roof</td><td>15 – 25 years</td><td>Curling, granules in gutters, bald spots</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Architectural shingle roof</td><td>25 – 30 years</td><td>Cracking, lifted edges after storms</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Metal roof</td><td>40 – 70 years</td><td>Loose fasteners, faded coating</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LVP / vinyl plank floor</td><td>15 – 25 years</td><td>Worn finish, lifting seams, gaps</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Carpet (rental grade)</td><td>5 – 8 years</td><td>Matting in walkways, stains that will not clean</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hardwood floor</td><td>25+ years (refinishable)</td><td>Deep scratches, gray or worn finish</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dishwasher</td><td>9 – 12 years</td><td>Poor cleaning, leaks, rust inside</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Garbage disposal</td><td>8 – 12 years</td><td>Jams, leaks, persistent odor</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Exterior paint</td><td>5 – 10 years</td><td>Fading, chalking, peeling at trim</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Caulk and sealant</td><td>~5 years</td><td>Cracking, gaps, mildew lines</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wood deck</td><td>15 – 20 years (with upkeep)</td><td>Soft boards, loose railings, gray wood</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Why lifespan matters more for landlords</h2>
  <p>
    On your own home, a failing water heater is an inconvenience. In a rental,
    it is a tenant call, possible water damage, and an emergency repair bill
    all at once. Knowing that your units are running 10-year-old water heaters
    or 20-year-old roofs lets you budget replacements before they become
    crises, and it protects you at move-out: you cannot fairly charge a tenant
    for a carpet that was already at the end of its life. See how age factors
    into <a href="/articles/wear-and-tear-vs-damage-tennessee/">wear and tear
    versus damage in Tennessee</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Maintenance changes these numbers</h2>
  <div class="takeaway">
    <p>
      Every range above has a top and a bottom, and maintenance decides which
      one you get. An HVAC system with annual service reaches 15 years; a
      neglected one dies at 10. A water heater flushed yearly outlasts one
      that is never touched by years. The cheapest way to extend a system is
      to service it on schedule, which is the entire argument for
      <a href="/articles/annual-property-maintenance-checklist/">an annual
      maintenance calendar</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>When a system is near the end</h2>
  <p>
    Once a system passes the middle of its lifespan and starts needing
    repairs, the math shifts. A single repair on a 6-year-old AC is worth it;
    the third repair on a 14-year-old unit usually is not. That decision has
    its own thresholds, which we break down in
    <a href="/articles/repair-or-replace-hvac-roof-water-heater/">repair or
    replace: a cost guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Track the age of the big systems in every property, service them on
    schedule to reach the top of these ranges, and plan replacements before
    failures plan them for you. Upkeep handles
    <a href="/services/hvac/">HVAC</a>, <a href="/services/roofing/">roofing</a>,
    plumbing, and <a href="/services/flooring/">flooring</a> across Middle
    Tennessee, and we will flag what is aging on every visit. One
    <a href="/work-order/">service request</a> starts the conversation.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
